    "reason": "5 attempts used (of 8), 3 remaining. Semantics established: ClearMyLife(MePlane,MeLocX,MeLocY,MeType,MeDir) unconditionally (matching src/recovered/wf_EnterNest-606488b329.c's call shape); plane defaults to 1 when the caller passes 0; bounds check 0<=x<=0x7f/0<=y<=0x3f for plane<=1 (yard) else 0<=x<=0x3f/0<=y<=0x3f (nest), silent return on failure; SetLife(plane,x,y,0xff) marks the destination; for type==0x60 (queen) a second SetLife(plane,x+Dx8[dir^4],y+Dy8[dir^4],0xfe) marks the trailing cell (reverse-direction idiom shared with GetExitDirB/R); My* globals then updated. Structural blocker, not an expression-shape guess: for every DGROUP near global reference (MeDir/MeType/MeLocY/MeLocX/MePlane at 0xcf50/0xcc84/0xce7e/0xcd88/0xce80) and every CONST selector-pool slot load (the two Dx8/Dy8 ES reloads at 0xc478/0xc47a), the ORIGINAL bytes are a bare literal DS-relative or immediate address with an EMPTY original loader-relocation list (evidence/recovery/workflow/jobs/MoveMyLife-f66a8c0d06/attempt05/candidate0000.diff.txt, e.g. target '0008 push word ptr [0xcf50]' vs candidate '0009 push <resolved fixup> segment10,offset53072' -- the numeric value is exactly right (53072==0xcf50) but the candidate's reference is a genuine relocatable extern fixup where the original has none). This is the same address-literal technique src/recovered/IsSamePlane.c used (#define MePlane (*(int near *)0xCE80)), but attempting it here (attempt failed pre-compile, no attempt consumed) was refused by the tool itself with 'literal-address pointer cast requires expert review', and grinder-lessons.md/AGENTS.md forbid numeric address casts for ordinary grinding. Plain 'extern int near MePlane;' (as used successfully in the admitted src/recovered/wf_EnterNest-606488b329.c and wf_CenterAnt-5b585d8f15.c, a DIFFERENT unit) instead produces a real relocation here across every attempt (1-5), regardless of ternary-vs-if/else assignment style, bounds-check branch order (plane>1 first vs plane<=1 first, both tried), or Dx8/Dy8 declared as one combined scalar+offset object (extern unsigned char far Dx8; AT()/DX() macro, matching FixExitMapB/R) vs two separate extern arrays (extern char far Dx8[]/Dy8[], matching GetExitDirB/R) -- the separate-array form did fix the 0xfe immediate and restored the expected two independent ES reloads structurally, but each reload is still a fixup where target has a bare CONST-slot literal. A second, apparently independent issue: target's same-segment near-rewritten calls to ClearMyLife/SetLife (push cs; call near, per the LINK same-segment-far-call rewrite rule) compile in my candidate as 'lcall <resolved fixup>' instead, in every attempt. A third, register-allocation-only issue: target keeps the post-ternary 'adjustedPlane' value in memory ([bp-2]) and always reloads it, using only SI throughout both bounds-check branches (enter 2,0; push si only); every candidate variant instead keeps it live in AX across the compare and needs an extra DI register in the first-compiled bounds-check branch (enter 2,0; push di; push si) -- unaffected by branch order or ternary-vs-if/else, so likely a downstream consequence of the DGROUP/fixup difference rather than an independent local issue. Best/most representative candidate: build/grind/agentF/_MoveMyLife.c == evidence/recovery/workflow/jobs/MoveMyLife-f66a8c0d06/attempt05/candidate0000.c (50/83 opcodes, cfg shape close but not exact, 0/19 fixups exact, 212 vs 217 target bytes); attempts 2-4 (plain extern, various branch/assignment orders) got 49/83 opcodes but 1/17 fixups exact.",
